    "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation",
    "problem": "Given that $f(x)={(2-5x)}^{3},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$",
    "worked_solution": "Let $g(x)=2-5x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{3}{)}^{′}=3{g}^{2}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2-5x{)}^{′}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-15(2-5x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
